 "name": "Weather front",
 "_note": "A front that physically moves through the world, built out of two things that already existed and one that is new. The thing that already existed is `spatial::FieldKind::Wave` -- a travelling wave with a planar geometry, a wavefront envelope and a speed in metres per second, with a WGSL twin and a CPU/GPU parity test. A planar pulse of wavelength 320 m and a 46 m envelope moving at 5 m/s along +x is not a metaphor for a squall line; it IS one, and nothing had to be written to make it move. The thing that is new is ADR-520's `emitMaskField`: a spawn survives with probability equal to the field's scalar at the spawn point, so the front's leading edge is a DENSITY GRADIENT and not a wall -- which is the difference between weather arriving and weather being switched on. ADR-025's Kill force could already delete particles outside a field, and that is exactly what it looks like: a vertical face of rain with clear air in front of it. Two systems subscribe to the same field with opposite signs. 'front-rain' takes the field as it is, so it rains inside the band. 'front-dust' takes 'front-inverse', the same wave with `invert` set, so the dry air ahead of and behind the squall carries lifted dust -- and because both read ONE field, the moment the rain arrives somewhere is the moment the dust there stops. That is §68's one-field-many-subscribers, and it is why this is a front rather than two unrelated animations that happen to be moving at the same speed. The band is at x = -60 + 5t; at t = 12 s it is over the camera, at t = 4 s it is 40 m away on the left.",
